"""Original general policy models and hypothetical currency data."""
import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]))
from authoring_composer import compose
P={
'01':('Influencing production and location','Government can influence business location, production and relationships between firms to pursue employment, development, environmental protection and fair competition. General tools include planning conditions, infrastructure, information, incentives and rules against harmful conduct. Explain why a problem needs attention and how the selected tool changes decisions. A location grant can reduce a site’s cost but cannot supply missing skills or customers by itself. Production standards can protect others but require implementation. Competition measures can address harmful agreements or market power without implying that every large firm is abusive. These are policy models, not a list of current Namibian rates or legal procedures.','A hypothetical government wants useful production in an area with weak transport links.','Improving access can remove a practical obstacle for firms and workers. An unrelated promotional slogan would not change delivery reliability.','how infrastructure can influence the proposed location and production decision'),
'02':('Assisting businesses','Government can assist regional development, small firms and exports through appropriate infrastructure, advice, skills support, eligible finance or grants, and information about markets. Regional support may address uneven opportunities; small firms may lack information or collateral; exporters may face unfamiliar customers and procedures. Assistance has an opportunity cost and can support weak projects if poorly targeted. Identify the actual constraint and conditions rather than assume a subsidy guarantees survival. Training may be on-the-job or off-the-job; choose it to address a capability gap rather than substitute for absent demand.','Fictional Omi Crafts has good products but lacks reliable information about nearby regional buyers and delivery options.','Relevant market and logistics advice may address its gap more directly than an unneeded machine grant. The firm must still confirm demand and affordability.','why assistance should match Omi’s information and delivery constraint'),
'03':('Evaluate intervention against the problem','Evaluate an intervention by linking its objective, mechanism, beneficiaries, resource cost and possible unintended effects. A grant may make useful investment possible but also use public funds that could support another need. Support may be temporary while an underlying weakness persists. Compare plausible alternatives such as information, infrastructure or a targeted capability improvement. Ask whether the measure actually changes the constraint and whether outcomes can be reviewed. Do not judge success from the amount spent or the number of applications alone.','A hypothetical scheme offers machines to fictional Hela Foods, but its existing equipment has spare capacity and its main problem is unreliable transport to customers.','Extra machines may add idle capacity without improving delivery. Targeted logistics support may be more relevant, subject to cost and evidence. Choose the intervention addressing access first rather than rewarding investment for its own sake.','whether a machine grant addresses Hela’s stated production constraint'),
'04':('Barriers to international trade','Trade barriers can include tariffs, quotas, embargoes, administrative obstacles and incompatible requirements, while transport, language and payment difficulties can also impede trade. A tariff is a tax on imports; a quota limits quantity; an embargo prohibits specified trade. A tariff can protect a local producer by raising an imported rival’s price, but can also raise input or consumer costs. A quota is not another percentage tax. Describe a barrier’s mechanism and affected parties rather than assume protection benefits everyone. The examples do not state a current tariff or restriction.','A hypothetical country taxes an imported product while a separate measure caps how many units may enter.','The tax is a tariff and the quantity cap a quota. A local input user may face higher costs from protection that helps a competing producer.','barriers to international trade',{'FEATURE':('State two policy barriers to international trade.','A tariff and an import quota.')}),
'05':('Currency conversion','An exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. Read its direction before calculating: if one foreign unit equals a stated number of N$, multiply foreign units by that N$ amount to convert into N$, or divide N$ by it to obtain foreign units. Show the units and state any assumption about fees. Do not reverse the rate or use a current market rate when a question supplies a hypothetical one. A conversion changes the currency expression, not automatically the underlying value of goods.','At a hypothetical rate US$1 = N$20, fictional Tula Goods must pay US$150; ignore fees.','N$ required = 150 × 20 = N$3,000. Conversely N$3,000 / 20 gives US$150. This is a teaching rate, not a quote for a real transaction.','',{'CALC':('Calculate the N$ needed by fictional Tula Goods to pay US$150 at the hypothetical rate US$1 = N$20. Ignore fees.','Multiply the foreign amount by N$ per foreign unit: 150 × 20 = N$3,000. Tula needs N$3,000 before any fee; multiplying is appropriate because each US$ costs twenty N$.')}),
'06':('Namibia and SADC','Namibia is a member of the Southern African Development Community, SADC. Regional cooperation and trading arrangements can improve access to markets, inputs and shared infrastructure. Preferential treatment depends on applicable conditions; regional membership does not mean every product and service faces no barrier. The [Namibia Trade Information Portal](https://namibiatradeportal.gov.na/tools-traders/rules-origin-rtas) explains that qualifying goods must meet origin requirements. Wider market access can support sales and choice, but Namibian producers also face stronger competition. Benefits depend on competitiveness, logistics, quality and actual demand, while adjustment can impose costs on some firms and workers. SADC is distinct from SACU; do not treat the organisations as interchangeable.','Fictional Nela Panels in Namibia considers a regional market where its product qualifies for the relevant preference. Customers still require reliable delivery and competitive quality.','Regional access is an opportunity, not a guaranteed order. Preferential treatment can improve its position while rivals gain access too. Use the opportunity if product, delivered cost and capacity fit actual buyers, and assess adjustment risks rather than assume membership benefits all firms equally.','the potential benefits and competitive risks of SADC membership for Namibia using Nela’s example')}
M={}
def m(parent,n,h,t,c,a,task):M[f'OBJ-6144-5.2.{parent}-{n:02}']=(h,t,c,a,task)
for n,(h,t,c,a,task) in enumerate([
('Local location decisions','At local level, public authorities can influence suitable land use, access, infrastructure and effects on neighbouring users. A business site needs to fit practical production and community requirements. Conditions or suitable industrial areas can reduce conflict, but a low-cost designated site is not automatically useful if customers, skills or utilities are inaccessible.','A hypothetical town offers serviced workshop space away from homes but connected to delivery roads.','The arrangement can reduce neighbouring disturbance and give firms usable access. Compare it with an isolated site rather than consider land price alone.','how serviced local premises can address access and neighbour effects'),
('National location decisions','National policy may seek more balanced regional development, employment and infrastructure. Assistance can influence firms’ site costs or access to skills and transport, but the investment must still be viable. A project that moves solely for a short subsidy may not remain once support ends. Assess durable capability and demand as well as initial incentives.','A hypothetical national programme improves transport to an underserved production area.','Reliable access may help firms supply customers and workers reach jobs, creating a more lasting benefit than a one-time incentive without infrastructure.','why national infrastructure can affect regional production choices'),
('International location decisions','Government can influence international location through the general investment environment, infrastructure, trade relationships and eligible assistance. Firms compare market access, skills, reliability and total costs across sites. Incentives have costs and conditions, so they should not be treated as guaranteed or as the sole reason a foreign operation is worthwhile.','A hypothetical investor compares a serviced Namibian site with a foreign location offering cheap rent but unreliable delivery.','Public investment in usable infrastructure can influence the choice, while rent alone does not establish competitiveness. Actual conditions and customer needs remain decisive.','how infrastructure and access can influence an international site comparison'),
('Intervention in production','Government can influence production through general safety, quality or environmental conditions and through support for skills, technology or infrastructure. Standards seek to protect affected people or maintain acceptable output; assistance seeks to remove capability constraints. Both require resources and monitoring. Do not assume every restriction is harmful or every grant improves productivity.','A hypothetical producer can meet an environmental condition by installing effective control, while its staff need relevant skills.','A combination of a clear condition and appropriate capability support may secure production with less harm. Giving money without checking its use may fail to achieve that result.','how a production condition and capability support can serve different purposes'),
('Relationships with other firms','Government may act to support fair competition and address harmful collusion, misuse of market power or practices that disadvantage others. The purpose can be to protect choice and fair opportunities rather than protect every competitor from any loss. Competition itself can press firms to improve. Evaluate the stated conduct and its effects instead of assuming any large enterprise or price reduction is abusive.','In a hypothetical market, suppliers agree to restrict supply together to raise the price faced by customers.','Scrutiny of the agreement addresses coordinated restriction rather than mere successful competition. A small rival’s complaint alone would not prove the same conduct.','why coordinated supply restriction can prompt government attention')],1):m('01',n,h,t,c,a,task)
for n,(h,t,c,a,task) in enumerate([
('Regional assistance','Regional assistance supports areas with limited economic opportunities through suitable infrastructure, advice, skills or conditional financial support. It can encourage productive activity and employment, but needs a realistic reason firms can operate there. Public cost and the risk of temporary relocation matter. Measure enduring outcomes rather than buildings or grant totals alone.','A hypothetical region has willing workers and demand access but lacks a reliable industrial electricity connection.','Improving that connection can remove a stated obstacle. A marketing campaign about the region would not supply the missing utility.','why regional assistance should address the missing electricity connection'),
('Assistance to small firms','Small firms can face limited finance, management time, information and access to specialist support. Government assistance can include advice, relevant development opportunities, suitable premises or eligible funding. Match it to the specific weakness and check whether demand and operations are viable. Support does not eliminate business risk or establish a right to indefinite subsidy.','Fictional Eya Repairs has customers and skills but weak cash planning.','Practical financial-planning advice can help identify payment timing and avoid an unaffordable commitment. An unrelated equipment grant may not address the problem.','how advice can support Eya’s actual small-business constraint'),
('Assistance to exporters','Export assistance can provide market information, advice about relevant requirements, trade contacts and support for logistics or finance where eligible. It can reduce information barriers but cannot guarantee foreign orders or remove all transport and payment risk. Exporting services may involve different access and delivery arrangements from sending physical goods. Confirm the opportunity and costs before committing.','Fictional Ota Crafts considers a nearby regional market but does not know buyer requirements or delivery costs.','Market and logistics information helps assess suitability. A large export target without those details would be a weak basis for investment.','how targeted information can help Ota assess an export opportunity')],1):m('02',n,h,t,c,a,task)
case={
'objectives':['OBJ-6144-5.2.02','OBJ-6144-5.2.03','OBJ-6144-5.2.04','OBJ-6144-5.2.06'],
'facts':'Fictional Hela Crafts in Namibia has spare capacity and an offering potentially suitable for regional buyers. It lacks market and delivery information. A hypothetical assistance programme can provide advice and contacts; another offers unneeded extra equipment. Relevant trade preferences have conditions, and no current tariff rate is supplied.',
'alternative_facts':'Fictional Nara Panels in Namibia has suitable spare capacity, dependable delivery options and verified interest from eligible regional buyers at a feasible delivered price. It compares a limited regional export trial with staying solely local, using available market-information support.',
'changed':'For part (b) only, reliable transport becomes unavailable so the required regional delivery deadline cannot be met. Other facts remain unchanged.',
'p1_questions':'a(i) State two ways government can assist a producer. [2]\na(ii) State one potential regional-trade opportunity and one risk for Namibia. [2]\nb State two reasons government may assist businesses. [2]\nc Explain whether advice or extra equipment better addresses Hela’s constraint. [6]\nd Evaluate using regional trading opportunities as a reason for Hela to export immediately. [8]',
'p1_answers':'a(i) Relevant market advice and usable infrastructure. a(ii) Wider potential markets and stronger import competition. b Supporting regional development and employment. c Spare capacity means extra machines do not address the known gap. Advice and contacts can supply buyer and logistics evidence, helping Hela assess delivered cost and demand. Assistance is worthwhile if relevant, though it still uses resources and needs review. d Regional access can provide opportunities but preferences are conditional and buyers still require suitable products and delivery. Hela lacks information, so immediate substantial export commitment is unjustified. Use advice and verify a feasible trial first; membership alone does not create orders or eliminate trade barriers.',
'p2_questions':'a Explain how assistance should address Hela’s constraint and what limits should be recognised. [8]\nb Evaluate the implications of regional trading opportunities for a producer and consumers in Namibia. [12]',
'p2_answers':'a Market and delivery advice supplies missing evidence while existing spare capacity can serve a verified opportunity. Equipment support might add idle resources and public cost without solving access. Advice quality and actual demand still require assessment; neither programme guarantees sales. b Producers may gain wider markets and input choices, while consumers may gain variety and competitive prices. Local firms also face stronger rivals, possible loss of sales and adjustment for workers. Preferences depend on applicable arrangements and qualifying conditions, not simply a SADC label. Namibia’s benefits therefore depend on firms’ capability, logistics and responses as well as market access. Support competitiveness and informed participation while assessing affected groups; do not claim every producer or consumer gains equally.',
'essay_question':'Evaluate Nara’s limited regional trial against remaining solely local under the original facts. Give a concise plan. [12]',
'essay_answer':'Opportunity: verified interest, feasible delivered price, spare capacity and dependable logistics support a controlled trial. Assistance: use relevant information and check applicable preference conditions. Risks: competition, payment and dependence on a new market remain. Alternative: local-only focus avoids some complexity but forgoes the evidenced opportunity. Judgement: try a limited export commitment and monitor paid demand, costs and service; regional membership is an enabling context rather than a guarantee.',
'case_questions':'a Evaluate Nara’s trial under the original facts. [12]\nb Evaluate it after the transport constraint changes. Explain whether the original recommendation holds. [12]',
'case_answers':'a A limited trial uses existing capacity and verified market interest with feasible delivery, while retaining caution about competition and payment. Information support can help, but the producer remains responsible for assessing actual terms. Prefer the trial over an unnecessary permanent commitment. b The required deadline is now impossible with stated transport, so the original logistics condition fails. Postpone or renegotiate the trial and investigate a reliable alternative before accepting orders. Regional preferences and buyer interest do not physically deliver goods. The recommendation changes because access is no longer feasible, while the potential regional opportunity remains worth investigating.'}
if __name__=='__main__':compose('5.2',P,M,case,extra_evidence={'OBJ-6144-5.2.06': [{'source_id': 'https://namibiatradeportal.gov.na/tools-traders/rules-origin-rtas', 'locator': 'Official primary-source clarification checked 27 October 2026; linked in the teaching text.', 'support_type': 'paraphrase'}]})
